Yes, Vue 3 introduces a new syntax called the `<script setup>` syntax, which makes it easier to work with the Composition API in Vue Single File Components (SFCs).

Instead of using a traditional `export default` object, you can define the component's logic by wrapping the `<script>` tag with the `setup` attribute in a Vue SFC. This new syntax enables a more concise way to write components, and it also results in better type inference when using TypeScript.

Here's a simple example of using the `<script setup>` syntax in a Vue 3 component:

```html
<template>
  <div>
    <h1>{{ greeting }}</h1>
    <button @click="increment">Count: {{ count }}</button>
  </div>
</template>

<script setup>
import { ref, computed } from 'vue';

const count = ref(0);
const greeting = computed(() => `Hello, Vue 3!`);

function increment() {
  count.value++;
}
</script>
```

With the `<script setup>` syntax, you automatically expose all the variables and functions used in the template without needing the `return` object inside a `setup` function. This makes the component code shorter and more concise. Note that this syntax is only available in Vue 3.2 and later versions.

